00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.800
Many of you have heard of this.
It's like an idea that if you

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.220
took a high school stats class,
it would have come up.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:42.000
But it turns out that it's
closely related to the simple

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.600
linear regression line

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:45.060
that we've seen.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:48.060
So the correlation coefficient,

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.950
this is in chapter 2.4.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:59.540
is denoted with the letter R,

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:04.540
measures the strength

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:08.320
of the linear,

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.330
that's important here,

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:19.920
association between x and

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:20.880
y.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:24.700
Did someone remind me, if you've
seen this before?

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.460
Again, it's totally fine if you
have not.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:29.100
What are the largest and
smallest possible values of this

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:30.100
thing?

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.340
1 and negative 1.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:35.340
Good.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:40.360
So no matter the data set,

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:42.260
the correlation coefficient

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.860
is between negative 1 and 1.
This is different than mean

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.760
squared error, right? Mean
squared error could be

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.260
arbitrarily big.

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:51.240
And the units of mean squared
error are the units of y

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.240
squared.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:53.880
So for example,

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.020
for these models here,

00:14:56.020 --> 00:14:59.060
their mean squared errors will
be in minutes squared, because

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.540
the y values are in minutes.
They're commute times in

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:02.360
minutes.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.420
And then their actual minus
predicted y squared.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.900
So the mean squared errors would
be in minutes squared.

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:11.720
The correlation coefficient is
not in any units.

00:15:11.780 --> 00:15:15.280
It's just a number that ranges
between negative 1 and 1.

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:17.680
What does positive 1 mean? A 1,

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.820
positive 1 means if you drew a
scatter plot of x and y,

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:25.040
the points would lie exactly on
a straight line.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:27.540
Everything lies exactly on a
straight line.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:28.980
But in addition to that,

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.730
that straight line has a
positive slope.

00:15:32.740 --> 00:15:34.260
So I'm doing this, but to me,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:35.720
that should be drawing this for
you,

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.020
positive slope.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:41.940
A negative 1 correlation means
the points lie exactly on a

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.360
straight line with a negative
slope.

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.900
Close to 0 means the points
don't look like they lie on a

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:50.020
line at all,

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:52.340
and values in between are, well,
something in between.

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:55.140
So here are some examples.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:58.120
In this top left one,

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.560
it doesn't really seem like
there's a pattern between the

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.840
x's and y's.
You could argue that maybe they

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:05.860
fit a circle, but that's not a
line, and so the correlation is

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:07.240
pretty close to 0.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:10.990
It's very, very, very slightly
positive,

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:13.440
but essentially it's 0.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.840
In the second one, it seems like
the points lie almost exactly on

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.320
a straight line, not perfectly,
because there is no line that

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.360
passes through all of these.

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:27.760
But it's pretty close to being
the case that all of these

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.580
points lie on a negatively
sloped line, which is why the

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:32.760
correlation coefficient is
almost negative 1.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.010
It's negative 0.993.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:37.960
The bottom left one is the most
confusing,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:42.460
because there is a very clear
pattern between the x and y.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:43.580
Like,

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:46.680
obviously, it seems like there's
this absolute value v shape

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.100
along which the x's and y's
fall.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:52.340
But remember, the point is the
correlation coefficient measures

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.980
how much it looks like the
scatter plot is one straight

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:55.980
line.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:00.080
And it doesn't look like that at
all in the bottom left.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.340
There's no one straight line
that would fit this data well.

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:05.180
If you extend to two straight
lines, then maybe.

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:08.760
But that's not what we're asking
here,

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:12.180
which is why the correlation in
the bottom left is very weak as

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:13.180
well.

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:16.940
And because it's slightly
negative,

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:19.720
the formula thinks, and we're
going to look at the formula in

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:22.900
a second, thinks the best
fitting line would be sloped

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:23.740
negatively.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:26.000
And then the bottom right, it
seems like the points are going

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.620
up and to the right, which is
why the correlation coefficient

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:31.260
is positive rather than
negative.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.280
But it's not,

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:36.520
it's positive, but not close to
1, right? It's 0.6,

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.220
which means it's up and to the
right, but there's still a lot

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:41.640
of vertical spread.
The trend isn't as clear as it

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.140
could be.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:46.160
Are we OK with this intuitive
understanding of how the

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.410
correlation coefficient works?

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:49.680
OK.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:50.240
Now,

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:52.420
I sort of implicitly said this
here,

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:55.880
but do you agree that it feels
like it should be the case that

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.820
if the correlation is positive
between x and y,

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:01.240
then the slope of the best
fitting line should also be

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:02.240
positive?

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.200
And if the correlation is
negative,

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:05.920
well, then the slope of the best
fitting line should also be

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:06.800
negative.

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.020
Do we agree?

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:08.680
OK.
OK,

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:13.620
so it turns out that there's a
way to express,

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.960
and I'll put this two slides in
the future,

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:20.960
the slope of the best fitting
line,

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:22.840
again, assuming we're using
squared loss here.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.980
This is all under the guise that
we're using squared loss.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:27.480
This slope,

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:33.060
which has many equal forms,

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:37.940
this is just the default one
that I think of.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:45.160
Lots of other forms.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.460
There's also a way to write it
in terms of the correlation

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:50.440
coefficient,

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:51.440
R.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:55.360
And I really like this formula
because it's much more concise

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:59.110
than these ones that have
summations in them.

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.060
It is the correlation
coefficient

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:06.180
times the standard deviation of
the y values over the standard

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:09.180
deviation of the x-values.

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:13.800
So this right away tells you
that if the correlation is

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:14.620
positive,

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:15.900
the slope is positive.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:18.420
If the correlation is negative,
the slope is negative.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:19.740
Why is that?

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:22.740
Well, these standard deviations.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:37.900
They can't be negative.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.440
So standard deviation of y is
positive, standard deviation of

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:41.660
x is positive.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:43.980
Can someone remind me, how do
you define the standard

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.660
deviation of y? This has come up
in homework one,

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:49.740
but I don't think we directly
discussed it here.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:52.720
What is the standard deviation
of the y values?

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:56.470
How would I calculate that?

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:02.100
It's the average distance of any
y point from y bar.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:08.080
It's close to that, but it's
subtly different.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:14.100
I'm going to start by taking 1
over n times the

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.620
sum, where i goes from 1 to n,
of yi minus y bar.

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:19.240
Indeed, it's related to that,
but those distances are supposed

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.840
to be squared.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:23.060
And then the standard deviation

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:27.340
takes the square root of all of
that.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:30.120
So this is a little different
than the formula you said,

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:34.880
because the average distance
from yi to y bar would not

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:38.000
involve a squared here, but that
would have an absolute value.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:43.000
And a key algebraic fact we need
to remember here is that this

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:47.640
square root doesn't cancel out
these squares.

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:51.220
The entire average, or this
entire summation, needs to be

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.300
evaluated first, and then you
square root the result.

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:55.440
So this is how we define the
standard deviation.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:58.380
And the thing inside of this
square root here, What is that

00:20:58.380 --> 00:20:59.880
called again?

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.700
The variance,

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:03.560
which is,

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.560
again, something you played
around with in homework one.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:12.270
So depending on the question
you're working on,

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:17.160
this correlation coefficient
version of the formula may be

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.910
more convenient to reason about
the situation,

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.160
which is why I think it's useful
to know.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:24.840
But this, of course,

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:27.560
I sort of skipped a step.
and I told you that there's a

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:30.200
way of expressing the line with
the correlation coefficient,

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.580
but I never really told you how
the correlation coefficient was

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:33.580
defined.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:35.960
Now, if you look at this,

00:21:35.960 --> 00:21:38.480
it seems like the correlation
coefficient has to involve some

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.060
sort of summation,

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.500
because it seems like we're
wrapping a lot of the behavior

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:46.250
of these summations into the
correlation coefficient.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.280
So how do we define the
correlation coefficient?

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.280
There is a formula for it, but
then I'll tell you the English

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.480
way of interpreting it.

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:59.860
It also involves a 1 over n
times the sum,

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:02.740
where i goes from 1 to n,

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:08.260
of xi minus x bar times

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.960
yi minus y bar.

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:16.660
But each of these are divided by
the standard deviations of the

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:18.160
respective variables.

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.660
So this is a formula.
And,

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:25.200
well, sure, you could memorize
it.

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:27.480
There's an English way of
interpreting this that you could

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:31.980
say to someone who hasn't taken
this class.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:34.400
I want you to try and think
creatively of the English way of

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:35.900
saying this.

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:39.620
OK, I'm here.
So how many people here took

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:41.120
EECS 280?

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:43.840
And if you took EECS 280, you
probably went on their website

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:45.960
and went to that grade
calculator thing where you type

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:48.600
in your midterm grade and it
shows you some bell curve.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:52.260
What was going on there? Like,
how do they implement that?

00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.420
Jovanita? Z-score?

00:22:53.420 --> 00:22:53.960
Yeah.

00:22:53.960 --> 00:22:58.540
And how is that related to this?

00:22:58.540 --> 00:23:01.540
So he said z-score.
What is a z-score?

00:23:01.600 --> 00:23:02.600
Well,

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:04.560
I was going to say,

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:05.180
like,

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:07.500
our needs can multiply into
z-scores together.

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:08.140
Yeah.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.300
The key is that what I'm doing
here

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.400
with x and y individually is I'm
converting them to their

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.700
z-scores.
So what this is measuring is the

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.960
number of standard deviations

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:31.380
xi is above the mean of the x's.

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:39.560
And the second factor here is
the equivalent for the y.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:43.780
The number of standard
deviations yi is above

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:45.840
y bar.

00:23:45.940 --> 00:23:48.880
So with that understanding,

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:54.000
what I'm seeing here is that for
each value in my data set, so I

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.240
have my x's all written out and
my y's all written out, and you

00:23:57.240 --> 00:23:59.020
should think of each of these as
being points,

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:02.260
what I'm doing is I'm
multiplying what's called the z

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.000
score for each coordinate.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:09.740
So I convert x to its number of
standard deviations above x bar,

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:14.400
and I convert y to its number of
standard deviations above y bar.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.400
So now a positive value means
you're above average, and a

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:19.420
negative value means you're
below average.

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:24.280
Now what I do is I multiply
these z scores for x's,

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:27.720
my x's and my y's, and then I
take their average product.

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:32.220
So it's the average product of
the z scores of x and y.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:55.440
And another way that z-scores
are sometimes put

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:58.240
are standard units.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:05.090
So what does a z-score of 2
mean?

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:09.100
What does a z-score of 2 mean?

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:12.100
You're two standard deviations
above the mean?

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:12.880
Yeah,

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:17.180
a z-score of two, no matter what
distribution or variable you're

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:17.900
looking at,

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:21.640
just means the data point you're
looking at is two standard

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:24.580
deviations above its average,

00:25:24.580 --> 00:25:28.080
or above the average of the data
set it came from.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:31.220
And so why these are sometimes
called standard units is that

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:34.880
z-scores don't have any units.
Whereas in our example data set,

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:38.940
The x values are measured in
time.

00:25:38.940 --> 00:25:42.380
8.45
was some number of hours.

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:46.520
And the y's were measured in
minutes.

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:49.800
But notice the numerator and
denominator, if we look at just

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:53.080
yi minus y bar over standard
deviation of y,

00:25:53.080 --> 00:25:56.240
if we're using our commute times
example, this is in minutes,

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:57.760
and this is also in minutes.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:00.340
So the units of minutes get
divided out.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:04.840
And so this doesn't have any
units.

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:08.020
And so the correlation
coefficient is the average

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:13.400
product of x and y once both
columns of this data set have

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.060
been converted to z-scores.

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:19.980
Why does this make sense as a
formula for the correlation?

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:23.040
Well, here's roughly one way to
think about it.

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:26.000
What I've done is I've taken an
example data set,

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:29.340
and I've drawn these black bars,

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:33.480
a vertical one at the average x
value and a horizontal one at

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:36.280
the average y value.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:38.360
So this is average x,

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:39.860
average y.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:44.440
For a data set in which the
linear association looks

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:47.160
positive, meaning it looks like
it slopes upwards,

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:50.640
you'd expect most of the points
to be in the top right and

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:51.640
bottom left.

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:54.500
Most of the data should be here.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:58.540
And so the two factors in that
sum we just computed,

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:03.180
this is positive if xi is above
its mean, And this is positive

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:05.060
if yi is above its mean.

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:09.520
So if they're both usually above
their mean together,

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:13.280
or both usually below their mean
together, we're multiplying two

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:15.900
positive numbers, which is
positive,

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:18.680
or two negative numbers, which
is also positive.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:21.780
So up here is positive times
positive, and down here is

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:23.140
negative times negative.

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:26.840
So if most of the data isn't in
the top right or bottom left,

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:30.480
those products will usually be
positive, which is why the

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:32.520
average of them will be
positive.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.040
If, on the other hand, we had a
negative linear association,

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:38.520
well, then we'd expect most of
the data points to be in the top

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:40.620
left and bottom right.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:42.080
And if you're in the top left,

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:44.760
well, then if you multiply your
x-coordinate and y-coordinate,

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:48.510
once they've been centered here
by subtracting the mean,

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:50.880
one will be positive, one will
be negative.

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:53.620
Here, the y is above average,
the x is below average.

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:56.380
You multiply those coordinates,
you get a negative number.

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:58.780
And here, it's the opposite.
x is above average, y is below

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:00.160
average.
You multiply those coordinates,

00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:02.540
you get a negative number.
So the average of those products

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:04.320
would be negative.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:08.060
So this is one way to see why
the correlation coefficient has

00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:10.740
the definition that it does.

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:12.620
And as I said before,

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.520
the correlation coefficient

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:18.580
gives us another way of defining

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:23.080
the slope of the line that
minimizes mean squared error.

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:24.740
OK,

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:26.540
what questions with the
correlation coefficient do we

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:27.540
have?

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:33.650
You seem OK?

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:36.000
One last thing.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:37.800
Chapter 2.4
has some cool examples at the

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:40.300
