00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:50.280
And what it illustrates are four
data sets, that is pairs of x, y

00:28:50.280 --> 00:28:51.280
points,

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:53.700
in which the average x value,

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.950
average y value,

00:28:56.320 --> 00:29:01.120
standard deviation of x and y
and correlation coefficient are

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:02.600
all the same.

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:06.140
If you have just those five
numbers, that tells you what the

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:09.260
slope and intercept of the line
that minimizes mean squared

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:12.040
error is.
Because here, the slope only

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:13.640
depends on the correlation,

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:15.220
the standard deviation of y,

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.320
and the standard deviation of x.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:21.680
And the intercept depends on the
mean of y,

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:23.740
the slope, and the mean of x.

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.960
So if I give you the five
numbers I've highlighted here,

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:27.960
r,

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:30.160
standard deviation of x,

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:31.560
standard deviation of y,

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:32.920
mean of x, mean of y,

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.420
then you can draw the line of
best fit.

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:35.880
That's all you need.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:38.820
But what I'm showing you here is
that the data set could look

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:39.940
very different.

00:29:39.940 --> 00:29:42.480
Like you could have two very
different looking data sets

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.620
that share those five
quantities.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:47.200
So just like knowing a bunch of
numbers and crunching a bunch of

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:48.420
numbers is not enough,

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:50.720
it's important when you are
working with real data to try

00:29:50.720 --> 00:29:52.560
and visualize it as best as you
can.

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.300
Because these data sets look
