which is measuring the length of
a vector,

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:44.880
what is the most natural way to
measure the length of this

00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:46.380
vector u?

00:58:47.260 --> 00:58:47.880
Yeah, Bella.

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:49.840
You can use the distance
formula.

00:58:49.840 --> 00:58:51.640
Yeah, or the Pythagorean
theorem,

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:56.140
our good old friend, Which says
that this vector

00:58:57.200 --> 00:59:02.260
Looks like the hypotenuse of a
right-angled triangle whose legs

00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:05.080
have lengths three and one

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:08.000
What does the Pythagorean
theorem tell us?

00:59:08.000 --> 00:59:12.840
It tells us the length of u is
the square root of 3 squared

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:15.090
plus 1 squared

00:59:15.260 --> 00:59:19.480
Which is the square root of 10?
Sound familiar?

00:59:19.480 --> 00:59:20.480
Okay

00:59:20.520 --> 00:59:26.100
The notation we will use for the
length of a vector is this.

00:59:27.980 --> 00:59:30.980
These double vertical bars.

00:59:31.980 --> 00:59:34.800
In some other classes, they may
just use a single vertical bar,

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:38.400
but I like using the double
vertical bars to emphasize that

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:41.120
this is not the same as the
absolute value of a number.

00:59:41.120 --> 00:59:42.880
It's like a different operation,

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:45.320
and it's measuring the length of
a vector.

00:59:45.320 --> 00:59:49.820
Now, there are lots of other
names that this length goes by,

00:59:50.080 --> 00:59:51.700
there's length,

00:59:53.840 --> 00:59:55.100
magnitude,

00:59:58.540 --> 00:59:59.980
and norm.

00:59:59.980 --> 01:00:02.760
These all refer to the same
idea.

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:05.000
And magnitude may be one that
you're familiar with from

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:07.760
physics class, because there
they will have defined a vector

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:11.260
as being an object with a
magnitude and a direction.

01:00:11.260 --> 01:00:12.480
This may be familiar.

01:00:12.480 --> 01:00:14.960
And the direction of a vector is
something we're going to talk

01:00:14.960 --> 01:00:16.980
about more on Thursday.

01:00:16.980 --> 01:00:17.620
OK,

01:00:17.620 --> 01:00:19.660
these all refer to the same
thing.

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:20.360
Now,

01:00:20.360 --> 01:00:24.420
let me comment on the idea of a
norm here.

01:00:24.420 --> 01:00:27.680
What we're using here is one way
of measuring how long a vector

01:00:27.680 --> 01:00:28.680
is,

01:00:29.060 --> 01:00:30.660
which is using the Pythagorean
theorem,

01:00:30.660 --> 01:00:31.660
which

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:34.940
we've established follows rules
of geometry that we're familiar

01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:35.900
with.

01:00:35.900 --> 01:00:38.400
It is not the only way we could
have measured the length of this

01:00:38.400 --> 01:00:39.400
vector.

01:00:39.940 --> 01:00:42.520
What is another way you could
have measured the length of this

01:00:42.520 --> 01:00:43.260
vector?

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:45.300
It would be a different formula,
not equal to, And it wouldn't

01:00:45.300 --> 01:00:46.860
give you the square root of 10.

01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:50.260
But it would be like there's an
alternative way of measuring how

01:00:50.260 --> 01:00:52.500
long this vector is.

01:00:52.500 --> 01:00:57.750
Anyone think of one, or have you
seen one before?

01:01:01.180 --> 01:01:05.020
Another way you could measure
the length of a vector is using

01:01:05.020 --> 01:01:07.920
what's called its Manhattan
distance,

01:01:07.920 --> 01:01:12.680
which is counting the number of
units you have to move left and

01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:15.700
right plus the number of units
you have to move up and down to

01:01:15.700 --> 01:01:18.320
get from the end of the vector,

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:20.220
the beginning of the vector to
the end.

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:23.140
So here, that would be 3 plus 1,
which is 4.

01:01:23.140 --> 01:01:24.700
Why is it called Manhattan
distance? Well, if you've been

01:01:24.700 --> 01:01:26.240
to New York, you know.

01:01:26.240 --> 01:01:28.920
And most of it, the streets are
organized like a nice grid.

01:01:28.920 --> 01:01:31.200
And to get between one point and
another point, you have to walk

01:01:31.200 --> 01:01:33.140
left and right and then walk up
and down.

01:01:33.140 --> 01:01:34.800
That's what this is named after.

01:01:34.800 --> 01:01:36.880
That's one way.
There are several other ways you

01:01:36.880 --> 01:01:37.500
could

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:42.000
measure the length of a vector.
And if you look at chapter 3.2,

01:01:43.360 --> 01:01:47.500
it describes other

01:01:47.500 --> 01:01:51.520
ways of measuring length.

01:01:55.480 --> 01:02:00.740
The fact that this is using the
regular Pythagorean theorem way,

01:02:02.340 --> 01:02:06.440
sometimes to make that super
clear, we will write a subscript

01:02:06.440 --> 01:02:11.200
2 here.
And all that means is that these

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:13.700
individual components are being
squared.

01:02:13.700 --> 01:02:16.580
But this is the default way.
If we don't give any more

01:02:16.580 --> 01:02:19.440
details on how we are measuring
the length, it is by using the

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:20.800
Pythagorean theorem.

01:02:20.800 --> 01:02:22.240
But wait, you may say.

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:24.600
This is the Pythagorean theorem
that you've heard of only works

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:25.780
in two dimensions.

01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:28.260
What about vectors with three
numbers or four numbers or five

01:02:28.260 --> 01:02:29.260
numbers?

01:02:30.520 --> 01:02:34.100
I want you to guess how the
formula extends, But at least in

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:35.600
three dimensions,

01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:40.080
we can draw another visual.

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:44.700
What I've drawn here in pink is
the vector 6,

01:02:44.700 --> 01:02:45.920
negative 2,

01:02:45.920 --> 01:02:46.920
3.

01:02:47.680 --> 01:02:50.900
And what I've done here is I've
given,

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:54.720
as an expression, the most
natural extension of the length

01:02:54.720 --> 01:02:57.780
formula to a vector with three
things in it.

01:02:57.780 --> 01:03:01.580
It's the square root of the sum
of the numbers in it

01:03:01.580 --> 01:03:02.580
squared.

01:03:03.040 --> 01:03:04.720
Do you agree that that's like a
natural extension of the

01:03:04.720 --> 01:03:06.220
Pythagorean theorem?

01:03:06.580 --> 01:03:07.240
OK.

01:03:07.240 --> 01:03:09.220
The fact that I have a negative
2 there,

01:03:09.220 --> 01:03:11.320
it could have been positive 2,
it doesn't matter.

01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:14.480
It's just easier to define the
formula in terms of the

01:03:14.480 --> 01:03:15.600
components themselves.

01:03:15.600 --> 01:03:17.800
But why does this measure the
length of this vector, 6,

01:03:17.800 --> 01:03:20.040
negative 2, 3?

01:03:20.040 --> 01:03:20.820
OK.

01:03:20.820 --> 01:03:23.540
The cop-out answer would be,
because I said it is, because

01:03:23.540 --> 01:03:24.800
that's the definition.

01:03:24.800 --> 01:03:27.360
But we can think about this
geometrically as well.

01:03:27.360 --> 01:03:31.780
So if you play around with this
picture, What you'll notice is

01:03:31.780 --> 01:03:36.480
that there are two right angle
triangles at play here.

01:03:36.700 --> 01:03:39.540
If I take this bird's eye view
where I don't have any depth

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:42.400
perception, I can't look at
height here, I have this first

01:03:42.400 --> 01:03:44.650
right angle triangle.

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:49.060
One of its legs has a length of
six and the other has a length

01:03:49.060 --> 01:03:50.040
of two.

01:03:50.040 --> 01:03:52.960
And notice six and two, these
lengths are coming from the x

01:03:52.960 --> 01:03:56.500
and y axis coordinates of the
vector six, negative two, and

01:03:56.500 --> 01:03:57.500
three,

01:03:57.500 --> 01:03:58.840
right? This 2,

01:03:58.840 --> 01:04:01.480
the 2 that's running
horizontally on the page here,

01:04:01.480 --> 01:04:03.000
is going from the point 0,

01:04:03.000 --> 01:04:07.220
0, 0 in 3D to 0, negative 2, 0.

01:04:07.220 --> 01:04:09.560
But it still has a length of 2.

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:12.660
And the taller leg has a length
of 6.

01:04:12.660 --> 01:04:15.240
Do we see where the numbers 2
and 6 are coming from in the

01:04:15.240 --> 01:04:18.960
context of 6, negative 2, and 3?

01:04:18.960 --> 01:04:21.760
So this right angle triangle,
the one that's in this shaded

01:04:21.760 --> 01:04:25.020
blue here, it has a hypotenuse.
And what is the length of its

01:04:25.020 --> 01:04:26.020
hypotenuse?

01:04:29.780 --> 01:04:32.060
What is the length of the
hypotenuse of the right angle

01:04:32.060 --> 01:04:33.280
triangle we're looking at?

01:04:33.280 --> 01:04:34.520
The blue one.

01:04:34.520 --> 01:04:35.320
Yeah, that's that one.

01:04:35.320 --> 01:04:35.960
Square root of 40.

01:04:35.960 --> 01:04:38.120
Square root of 40, right? We
just apply the Pythagorean

01:04:38.120 --> 01:04:38.960
theorem

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:42.640
to a right angle triangle whose
side lengths are 2 and 6.

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:46.200
So now we've got the hypotenuse
of this blue triangle is square

01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:47.620
root of 40.

01:04:47.620 --> 01:04:49.620
Now we're going to rotate here
and say,

01:04:49.620 --> 01:04:49.840
OK,

01:04:49.840 --> 01:04:51.120
forget about that one.

01:04:51.120 --> 01:04:53.660
Now we've got another right
angle triangle.

01:04:53.660 --> 01:04:56.960
And what are the side length
legs here, are the legs of the

01:04:56.960 --> 01:04:58.240
side lengths here,

01:04:58.240 --> 01:05:00.620
the lengths of the side lengths.

01:05:00.620 --> 01:05:01.960
What are those?

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:05.180
The two perpendicular legs here,
what are their lengths?

01:05:05.180 --> 01:05:07.060
Yeah? Square root of 40 and 3.

01:05:07.060 --> 01:05:07.800
Yeah.

01:05:07.800 --> 01:05:10.940
The longer leg here is the
square root of 4, which is the

01:05:10.940 --> 01:05:14.600
hypotenuse of the first triangle
we saw.

01:05:14.600 --> 01:05:17.080
Do we see that?

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:20.780
This leg that's labeled H behind
the pink wall here.

01:05:20.780 --> 01:05:23.480
That's square root of 40, and
the height is 3.

01:05:23.480 --> 01:05:26.220
And so what is the overall
length? Well, it's the square

01:05:26.220 --> 01:05:30.240
root of 40 plus 3 squared,

01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:32.140
which is the square root of 49,

01:05:32.140 --> 01:05:33.080
which is 7.

01:05:33.080 --> 01:05:35.400
If we just unwrap that formula,

01:05:35.400 --> 01:05:38.040
how did we get the 40 under the
square root?

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:41.320
Well, it was just 2 squared plus
6 squared.

01:05:41.320 --> 01:05:44.680
So the overall length is just
the square root of 2 squared

01:05:44.680 --> 01:05:46.960
plus 6 squared plus 3 squared,

01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:50.060
which is the square root of the
sum of the squares of the

01:05:50.060 --> 01:05:51.060
components,

01:05:51.540 --> 01:05:54.580
which is what we started with.

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:57.060
So do we see how the length of
this vector w

01:05:57.060 --> 01:06:00.760
is really just the square root
of the sum of the squares of the

01:06:00.760 --> 01:06:01.760
components?

01:06:02.160 --> 01:06:04.160
Square root of 6 squared plus
negative 2 squared plus 3

01:06:04.160 --> 01:06:05.160
squared.

01:06:06.060 --> 01:06:08.760
After class, I want you to come
to this picture and play around

01:06:08.760 --> 01:06:11.100
with it and make sense of it.
When we start thinking in three

01:06:11.100 --> 01:06:14.440
dimensions, it can be hard to
wrap your head around what's

01:06:14.440 --> 01:06:15.820
going on.

01:06:15.820 --> 01:06:17.460
And it would be easy to just
say, like, all right, well,

01:06:17.460 --> 01:06:18.760
here's the definition of the
length.

01:06:18.760 --> 01:06:19.900
Here's what it said in the
textbook.

01:06:19.900 --> 01:06:22.020
So I'm giving it to you.
But I want you to understand

01:06:22.020 --> 01:06:24.860
where these things come from.

01:06:24.860 --> 01:06:30.110
So this gives us a way of
defining

01:06:31.160 --> 01:06:36.410
the length of a vector in Rn.

01:06:38.060 --> 01:06:42.300
Can someone tell me what that
formula would look like?

01:06:42.300 --> 01:06:45.400
The length of a vector in Rn.

01:06:45.400 --> 01:06:46.240
Yeah, Allison.

01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:52.240
Square root of the sum of i
equals 1 to n

01:06:52.240 --> 01:06:56.960
of Vi squared.

01:06:57.260 --> 01:06:57.760
Good.

01:06:57.760 --> 01:07:01.060
Remember, the default
placeholder for the ith element

01:07:01.060 --> 01:07:03.260
in a vector is Vi.

01:07:03.260 --> 01:07:08.780
So this would be the square root
of the sum of Vi squared.

01:07:09.480 --> 01:07:11.500
And if I write that without
summation notation, it's going

01:07:11.500 --> 01:07:17.160
to look like V1 squared plus V2
squared plus through

01:07:17.160 --> 01:07:18.660
vn squared,

01:07:20.020 --> 01:07:20.960
OK?

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:24.680
And like I said, sometimes this
is called the L2 norm,

01:07:24.680 --> 01:07:27.680
which is the default.

01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:30.160
But chapter 3.2
tells you about some of the

01:07:30.160 --> 01:07:34.660
other norms, like the L1 norm,
for example.

01:07:34.660 --> 01:07:35.660
OK,

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:40.440
questions with the length of a
vector so far?

01:07:44.680 --> 01:07:46.180
Seem OK?

01:07:48.550 --> 01:07:49.520
Seem reasonable?

01:07:49.520 --> 01:07:50.520
OK.

01:07:50.900 --> 01:07:55.860
So there are two key operations
that vectors support.

01:08:05.750 --> 01:08:08.700
You can add them,

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:14.460
and you can multiply them by a
scalar.

01:08:16.140 --> 01:08:19.100
Notice I'm prefacing my
definition of multiplication

01:08:19.100 --> 01:08:22.100
here with scalar, scalar
multiplication.

01:08:22.300 --> 01:08:24.400
So what this will tell us how to
do

01:08:24.400 --> 01:08:28.580
is multiply a vector by a single
number.

01:08:28.580 --> 01:08:32.340
It turns out the idea of
multiplying a vector,

01:08:32.340 --> 01:08:33.780
there's lots of ways you can do
it.

01:08:33.780 --> 01:08:37.140
And next class, we will see ways
that you could multiply a vector

01:08:37.140 --> 01:08:38.020
with another.

01:08:38.020 --> 01:08:41.560
But it doesn't necessarily work
the way that you think it would.

01:08:41.560 --> 01:08:42.560
OK,

01:08:42.660 --> 01:08:45.160
so I want to start today by
introducing addition

01:08:45.160 --> 01:08:47.410
and scalar multiplication.

01:08:48.860 --> 01:08:49.500
OK,

01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:52.500
let's start with addition.

01:08:55.040 --> 01:08:57.980
Suppose I have two vectors,

01:08:57.980 --> 01:09:01.380
u and v,

01:09:02.420 --> 01:09:06.170
and both are in Rn.

01:09:06.460 --> 01:09:10.960
What is the practical meaning of
this?

01:09:12.640 --> 01:09:14.840
Why am I starting off by saying
this?

01:09:14.840 --> 01:09:16.820
They have the same number of
dimensions.

01:09:16.820 --> 01:09:17.200
Yeah,

01:09:17.200 --> 01:09:21.700
they have the same number of
components.

01:09:30.180 --> 01:09:35.980
This is an important disclaimer
to start with, because as you

01:09:35.980 --> 01:09:38.700
will see, it only makes sense to
add vectors when they both have

01:09:38.700 --> 01:09:40.240
the same number of things in
them.

01:09:40.240 --> 01:09:42.700
They both have five things, they
both have two things, they both

01:09:42.700 --> 01:09:44.950
have three things.

01:09:45.400 --> 01:09:50.650
Now, let's suppose I add u and
v.

01:09:54.160 --> 01:09:55.480
The result

01:09:55.480 --> 01:09:57.500
is a new vector.

01:09:57.500 --> 01:10:00.200
But what is in that new vector?

01:10:00.200 --> 01:10:01.700
Anyone know?

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:04.480
So you have to guess how this
works.

01:10:04.480 --> 01:10:06.100
If you add vectors u and v,

01:10:06.100 --> 01:10:10.600
what What are the components of
the new vector u plus v?

01:10:13.360 --> 01:10:15.610
u1 plus v1.
Good.

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:18.780
The components get added up
element-wise.

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:21.800
So the first component of the
new vector

01:10:21.800 --> 01:10:27.050
will be the first thing in u
plus the first thing in v.

01:10:27.460 --> 01:10:29.360
The second thing in the new
vector

01:10:29.360 --> 01:10:34.610
will be the second thing in u
plus the second thing in v.

01:10:36.780 --> 01:10:40.160
And the last thing in the new
vector will be the last thing in

01:10:40.160 --> 01:10:45.410
u plus the last thing in v.

01:10:46.160 --> 01:10:49.920
This definition tells you that u
plus v is also equal to some

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:51.420
other operation.

01:10:51.820 --> 01:10:55.570
What's it also equal to?

01:10:57.440 --> 01:11:00.580
It may seem like a little
pedantic or

01:11:00.580 --> 01:11:04.380
obvious,

01:11:04.920 --> 01:11:09.240
but it's important for us to
state this.

01:11:09.240 --> 01:11:11.940
Yes, it's also equal to v plus
u.

01:11:11.940 --> 01:11:15.040
Now you may be saying like yeah,
obviously u plus v equals v plus

01:11:15.040 --> 01:11:16.580
u That's like grade 2 math

01:11:16.580 --> 01:11:19.940
But the reason it's important to
spell this out here is because

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:22.980
soon we will see operations
where this is no longer the case

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:27.300
Up until now in your sort of
math

01:11:27.300 --> 01:11:30.040
You're like journey of learning
math almost every operation

01:11:30.040 --> 01:11:34.720
worked both ways I guess there's
one that doesn't, which is

01:11:34.720 --> 01:11:36.280
division.
2 divided by 3 is not the same

01:11:36.280 --> 01:11:37.400
as 3 divided by 2.

01:11:37.400 --> 01:11:40.280
But that feels a little more
intuitive to us.

01:11:40.280 --> 01:11:43.440
Addition does work both ways.
And what is actually the term

01:11:43.440 --> 01:11:44.940
for this?

01:11:47.560 --> 01:11:50.780
Vector addition is what?

01:11:52.980 --> 01:11:53.980
Commutative.

01:12:04.400 --> 01:12:05.400
Commutative,

01:12:05.540 --> 01:12:07.860
the property is called
commutativity,

01:12:07.860 --> 01:12:09.760
is one of those weird words that
you've heard of in math.

01:12:09.760 --> 01:12:12.010
There's also associativity,
distributivity.

01:12:12.140 --> 01:12:12.800
To me,

01:12:12.800 --> 01:12:15.420
whether something is commutative
is the most important of those

01:12:15.420 --> 01:12:17.200
because, as I said,

01:12:17.200 --> 01:12:19.660
we will soon see operations
where they are no longer

01:12:19.660 --> 01:12:22.380
commutative when you would
assume that they are.

01:12:22.380 --> 01:12:24.800
The way that I remember that
this is what commutative refers

01:12:24.800 --> 01:12:27.620
to is that it sort of relates to
our running example in the

01:12:27.620 --> 01:12:28.280
class.

01:12:28.280 --> 01:12:30.620
When you commute to work, you
also go back.

01:12:30.620 --> 01:12:33.340
So like going there and coming
back should be the same so like

